{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-94224" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The mass density function $\\lambda $ of a rectangular flat plate that occupies the region $D={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 2}$ is given by $\\lambda (x,y)=y{e}^{xy}.$ What is the total mass of the plate?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14066271/state.json" "file_sha256" "d4617ec956bc238ddfc138faf068e77a40b7ce8ce4e0f135276b656466fa1f96"} {"question" "q-94224" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The mass $M$ of a plate that occupies the region $D={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 2}$ with mass density $\\lambda (x,y)=y{e}^{xy}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ={∬}_{D}y{e}^{xy}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we express our double integral as a repeated integral:      \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}y{e}^{xy}\\,\\text{d}A & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}[{\\int }_{0}^{1}y{e}^{xy}\\,\\text{d}x]\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $x$, treating $y$ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{2}[{\\int }_{0}^{1}y{e}^{xy}\\,\\text{d}x]\\text{d}y & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}y[{\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{xy}\\,\\text{d}x]\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}[{e}^{xy}]_{0}^{1}\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}({e}^{y}-{e}^{0})\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}({e}^{y}-1)\\,\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we integrate with respect to $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{2}({e}^{y}-1)\\,\\text{d}y & =[{e}^{y}-y]_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =({e}^{2}-2)-(1-0) \\\\  & ={e}^{2}-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the mass of the plate is $M={e}^{2}-3.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14066271/state.json" "file_sha256" "d4617ec956bc238ddfc138faf068e77a40b7ce8ce4e0f135276b656466fa1f96"} {"question" "q-94224" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14066271/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "69c1da566dbedc787fd3cece82b0ecc66f9453f587bcf4e96a35c375c4cc13d7"} {"question" "q-94224" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14066271/state.json" "file_sha256" "d4617ec956bc238ddfc138faf068e77a40b7ce8ce4e0f135276b656466fa1f96"} {"question" "q-94224" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "3-{e}^{2}"] ["math" "e-3"] ["math" "{e}^{2}"] ["math" "{e}^{2}-3"] ["math" "{e}^{3}-2"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14066271/state.json" "file_sha256" "d4617ec956bc238ddfc138faf068e77a40b7ce8ce4e0f135276b656466fa1f96"} {"question" "q-94224" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{e}^{2}-3"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14066271/state.json" "file_sha256" "d4617ec956bc238ddfc138faf068e77a40b7ce8ce4e0f135276b656466fa1f96"} {"question" "q-94170" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The mass density function $\\lambda $ of a rectangular wooden flat plate that occupies the region $D={(x,y)\\,:\\,-2\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le 1}$ is given by $\\lambda (x,y)=6{x}^{2}y.$ What is the total mass of the plate?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14066271/state.json" "file_sha256" "d4617ec956bc238ddfc138faf068e77a40b7ce8ce4e0f135276b656466fa1f96"} {"question" "q-94170" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The mass $M$ of a plate that occupies the region $D={(x,y)\\,:\\,-2\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le 1}$ with mass density $\\lambda (x,y)=6{x}^{2}y$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ={∬}_{D}6{x}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we express our double integral as a repeated integral:      \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}6{x}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}A & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}[{\\int }_{-2}^{2}6{x}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}x]\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $x$, treating $y$ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}[{\\int }_{-2}^{2}6{x}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}x]\\text{d}y & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}6y[{\\int }_{-2}^{2}{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x]\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}6y{[\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}]}_{-2}^{2}\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}6y(\\frac{{2}^{3}}{3}-\\frac{(-2)^{2}}{3})\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}32y\\,\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we integrate with respect to $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}32y\\,\\text{d}y & =[16{y}^{2}]_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =16\\cdot ({1}^{2}-0) \\\\  & =16\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the mass of the plate is $M=16.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14066271/state.json" "file_sha256" "d4617ec956bc238ddfc138faf068e77a40b7ce8ce4e0f135276b656466fa1f96"} {"question" "q-94170" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14066271/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "69c1da566dbedc787fd3cece82b0ecc66f9453f587bcf4e96a35c375c4cc13d7"} {"question" "q-94170" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14066271/state.json" "file_sha256" "d4617ec956bc238ddfc138faf068e77a40b7ce8ce4e0f135276b656466fa1f96"} {"question" "q-94170" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "1"] ["math" "12"] ["math" "16"] ["math" "5"] ["math" "6"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14066271/state.json" "file_sha256" "d4617ec956bc238ddfc138faf068e77a40b7ce8ce4e0f135276b656466fa1f96"} {"question" "q-94170" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "16"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14066271/state.json" "file_sha256" "d4617ec956bc238ddfc138faf068e77a40b7ce8ce4e0f135276b656466fa1f96"} {"question" "q-94535" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the total charge $Q$ of a metal plate that occupies the region $D={(x,y):0\\le x\\le \\pi ,\\,0\\le y\\le 1}$ with charge density $\\rho (x,y)=y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14066271/state.json" "file_sha256" "d4617ec956bc238ddfc138faf068e77a40b7ce8ce4e0f135276b656466fa1f96"} {"question" "q-94535" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The total charge $Q$ of a plate that occupies the region $D={(x,y):0\\le x\\le \\pi ,\\,0\\le y\\le 1}$ with charge density $\\rho (x,y)=y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q & ={∬}_{D}y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we express our double integral as a repeated integral:\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)\\,\\text{d}A={\\int }_{0}^{1}[{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)\\,\\text{d}x]\\text{d}y\n$$\n\nNow, we evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $x$, treating $y$ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}[{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)\\,\\text{d}x]\\text{d}y & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}y[{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)\\,\\text{d}x]\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}y{[\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)}{y}]}_{0}^{\\pi }\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}[\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)]_{0}^{\\pi }\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}[\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi y)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡0]\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi y)\\,\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we integrate with respect to $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi y)\\,\\text{d}y & ={[-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi y)}{\\pi }]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi \\cdot 1)}{\\pi }+\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi \\cdot 0)}{\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }+\\frac{1}{\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\pi }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the charge of the plate is $Q=\\frac{2}{\\pi }.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14066271/state.json" "file_sha256" "d4617ec956bc238ddfc138faf068e77a40b7ce8ce4e0f135276b656466fa1f96"} {"question" "q-94535" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14066271/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "69c1da566dbedc787fd3cece82b0ecc66f9453f587bcf4e96a35c375c4cc13d7"} {"question" "q-94535" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14066271/state.json" "file_sha256" "d4617ec956bc238ddfc138faf068e77a40b7ce8ce4e0f135276b656466fa1f96"} {"question" "q-94535" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "2"] ["math" "2\\pi "] ["math" "\\frac{1}{2}"] ["math" "\\frac{2}{\\pi }"] ["math" "\\pi +1"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14066271/state.json" "file_sha256" "d4617ec956bc238ddfc138faf068e77a40b7ce8ce4e0f135276b656466fa1f96"} {"question" "q-94535" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{2}{\\pi }"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14066271/state.json" "file_sha256" "d4617ec956bc238ddfc138faf068e77a40b7ce8ce4e0f135276b656466fa1f96"} {"question" "q-94221" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The mass density function $\\lambda $ of a rectangular flat plate that occupies the region $D={(x,y)\\,:\\,1\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le 2}$ is given by $\\lambda (x,y)=\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}}.$ What is the total mass of the plate?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14066271/state.json" "file_sha256" "d4617ec956bc238ddfc138faf068e77a40b7ce8ce4e0f135276b656466fa1f96"} {"question" "q-94221" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The mass $M$ of a plate that occupies the region $D={(x,y)\\,:\\,1\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le 2}$ with mass density $\\lambda (x,y)=\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we express our double integral as a repeated integral:      \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}[{\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x]\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $x$, treating $y$ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{2}[{\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x]\\text{d}y & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{y}^{2}[{\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x]\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{y}^{2}{[-\\frac{1}{x}]}_{1}^{2}\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{y}^{2}(-\\frac{1}{2}-(-1))\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}\\,\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we integrate with respect to $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}\\,\\text{d}y & ={[\\frac{{y}^{3}}{6}]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{{2}^{3}}{6}-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the mass of the plate is $M=\\frac{4}{3}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14066271/state.json" "file_sha256" "d4617ec956bc238ddfc138faf068e77a40b7ce8ce4e0f135276b656466fa1f96"} {"question" "q-94221" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14066271/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "69c1da566dbedc787fd3cece82b0ecc66f9453f587bcf4e96a35c375c4cc13d7"} {"question" "q-94221" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14066271/state.json" "file_sha256" "d4617ec956bc238ddfc138faf068e77a40b7ce8ce4e0f135276b656466fa1f96"} {"question" "q-94221" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "3"] ["math" "4"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{2}"] ["math" "\\frac{3}{4}"] ["math" "\\frac{4}{3}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14066271/state.json" "file_sha256" "d4617ec956bc238ddfc138faf068e77a40b7ce8ce4e0f135276b656466fa1f96"} {"question" "q-94221" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{4}{3}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14066271/state.json" "file_sha256" "d4617ec956bc238ddfc138faf068e77a40b7ce8ce4e0f135276b656466fa1f96"} {"question" "q-94174" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The mass density function $\\lambda $ of a rectangular iron flat plate that occupies the region $D={(x,y)\\,:\\,{e}^{2}\\le x\\le {e}^{6},\\,0\\le y\\le 3}$ is given by $\\lambda (x,y)=\\frac{{y}^{3}}{x}.$ What is the total mass of the plate?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14066271/state.json" "file_sha256" "d4617ec956bc238ddfc138faf068e77a40b7ce8ce4e0f135276b656466fa1f96"} {"question" "q-94174" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The mass $M$ of a plate that occupies the region $D={(x,y)\\,:\\,{e}^{2}\\le x\\le {e}^{6},\\,0\\le y\\le 3}$ with mass density $\\lambda (x,y)=\\frac{{y}^{3}}{x}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{{y}^{3}}{x}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we express our double integral as a repeated integral:      \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\frac{{y}^{3}}{x}\\,\\text{d}A & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}[{\\int }_{{e}^{2}}^{{e}^{6}}\\frac{{y}^{3}}{x}\\,\\text{d}x]\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $x$, treating $y$ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{3}[{\\int }_{{e}^{2}}^{{e}^{6}}\\frac{{y}^{3}}{x}\\,\\text{d}x]\\text{d}y & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}{y}^{3}[{\\int }_{{e}^{2}}^{{e}^{6}}\\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x]\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}{y}^{3}[\\operatorname{ln}⁡x]_{{e}^{2}}^{{e}^{6}}\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}{y}^{3}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡{e}^{6}-\\operatorname{ln}⁡{e}^{2})\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}4{y}^{3}\\,\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we integrate with respect to $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{3}4{y}^{3}\\,\\text{d}y & =[{y}^{4}]_{0}^{3} \\\\  & ={3}^{4}-(0)^{4} \\\\  & =81\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the mass of the plate is $M=81.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14066271/state.json" "file_sha256" "d4617ec956bc238ddfc138faf068e77a40b7ce8ce4e0f135276b656466fa1f96"} {"question" "q-94174" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14066271/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "69c1da566dbedc787fd3cece82b0ecc66f9453f587bcf4e96a35c375c4cc13d7"} {"question" "q-94174" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14066271/state.json" "file_sha256" "d4617ec956bc238ddfc138faf068e77a40b7ce8ce4e0f135276b656466fa1f96"} {"question" "q-94174" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "10"] ["math" "32"] ["math" "5"] ["math" "50"] ["math" "81"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14066271/state.json" "file_sha256" "d4617ec956bc238ddfc138faf068e77a40b7ce8ce4e0f135276b656466fa1f96"} {"question" "q-94174" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "81"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14066271/state.json" "file_sha256" "d4617ec956bc238ddfc138faf068e77a40b7ce8ce4e0f135276b656466fa1f96"} {"question" "q-95141" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the total charge $Q$ of the lower half of the circular disk ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 9$ with charge density function $\\rho $ given by $\\rho (x,y)=y$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14066271/state.json" "file_sha256" "d4617ec956bc238ddfc138faf068e77a40b7ce8ce4e0f135276b656466fa1f96"} {"question" "q-95141" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The total charge $Q$ of  the lower half of the circular disk  $D={(x,y)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 9,\\,y\\le 0}$ with charge density $\\rho (x,y)=y$ is given by\n\n$$\nQ={∬}_{D}y\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nSince $y\\le 0,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 9\\,⟹\\,y\\ge -\\sqrt{9-{x}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nLet's sketch our region.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14066271/assets/af77552bd144fb70dfe8787060c0136f54955d20cdd2543391bd040b01a460d2.png)\n\nNotice that $D$ is a type I region.\n\n- We already know the intersection of the circle with the line $y=0.$ So, the $x$-limits are $-3\\le x\\le 3.$\n\n- To determine the lower and upper functions, we draw a vertical arrow through the region.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14066271/assets/32db7009737aa47c8b5a96da91d72b0db95530271f463a9f7d35f715793cbea8.png)\n\nAs a result, the integral is defined over the following type I region:\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,-3\\le x\\le 3,\\,-\\sqrt{9-{x}^{2}}\\le y\\le 0}\n$$\n\nSo, we can express the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}y\\,\\text{d}A={\\int }_{-3}^{3}{\\int }_{-\\sqrt{9-{x}^{2}}}^{0}y\\,dy\\,dx\n$$\n\nFirst, we evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $y,$ treating $x$ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q & ={\\int }_{-3}^{3}[{\\int }_{-\\sqrt{9-{x}^{2}}}^{0}y\\,dy]\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-3}^{3}{[\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}]}_{-\\sqrt{9-{x}^{2}}}^{0}\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-3}^{3}(0-\\frac{9-{x}^{2}}{2})\\,dx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-3}^{3}({x}^{2}-9)\\,dx\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-3}^{3}({x}^{2}-9)\\,dx & =\\frac{1}{2}{[\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}-9x]}_{-3}^{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[(9-27)-(-9+27)] \\\\  & =-18\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the total charge of the lower half of the circular disk is $Q=-18.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14066271/state.json" "file_sha256" "d4617ec956bc238ddfc138faf068e77a40b7ce8ce4e0f135276b656466fa1f96"} {"question" "q-95141" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14066271/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "69c1da566dbedc787fd3cece82b0ecc66f9453f587bcf4e96a35c375c4cc13d7"} {"question" "q-95141" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14066271/state.json" "file_sha256" "d4617ec956bc238ddfc138faf068e77a40b7ce8ce4e0f135276b656466fa1f96"} {"question" "q-95141" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-18"] ["math" "-6"] ["math" "-9"] ["math" "12"] ["math" "9"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14066271/state.json" "file_sha256" "d4617ec956bc238ddfc138faf068e77a40b7ce8ce4e0f135276b656466fa1f96"} {"question" "q-95141" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-18"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14066271/state.json" "file_sha256" "d4617ec956bc238ddfc138faf068e77a40b7ce8ce4e0f135276b656466fa1f96"} {"question" "q-38248" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A metal plate occupies the region $D={(x,y):-1\\le y\\le 1,\\,0\\le x\\le {y}^{2}}.$ The charge density of the plate is given by $\\rho (x,y)=x{y}^{2}.$ Find the total charge $Q$ of the plate." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14066271/state.json" "file_sha256" "d4617ec956bc238ddfc138faf068e77a40b7ce8ce4e0f135276b656466fa1f96"} {"question" "q-38248" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The total charge $Q$ of a plate that occupies the region $D$ with charge density $\\rho (x,y)=x{y}^{2}$ is given by\n\n$$\nQ={∬}_{D}x{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nLet's sketch our region.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14066271/assets/1cbe96aa4bf517302a2329db9f8eb2e9c56873e4d5a18cd5f1cd676543e97086.png)\n\nNotice that $D$ is a type II region. So, we can express the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}x{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{{y}^{2}}x{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y\n$$\n\nFirst, we evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $x$, treating $y$ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{y}^{2}[{\\int }_{0}^{{y}^{2}}x\\,\\text{d}x]\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{y}^{2}{[\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}]}_{0}^{{y}^{2}}\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{y}^{2}(\\frac{{y}^{4}}{2}-0)\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{y}^{6}\\,\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate with respect to $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{y}^{6}\\,\\text{d}y & =\\frac{1}{2}{[\\frac{{y}^{7}}{7}]}_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{{1}^{7}}{7}-(-\\frac{{1}^{7}}{7})) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{7}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the total charge of the plate is $Q=\\frac{1}{7}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14066271/state.json" "file_sha256" "d4617ec956bc238ddfc138faf068e77a40b7ce8ce4e0f135276b656466fa1f96"} {"question" "q-38248" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14066271/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "69c1da566dbedc787fd3cece82b0ecc66f9453f587bcf4e96a35c375c4cc13d7"} {"question" "q-38248" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14066271/state.json" "file_sha256" "d4617ec956bc238ddfc138faf068e77a40b7ce8ce4e0f135276b656466fa1f96"} {"question" "q-38248" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\frac{1}{3}"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{6}"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{7}"] ["math" "\\frac{2}{3}"] ["math" "\\frac{2}{7}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14066271/state.json" "file_sha256" "d4617ec956bc238ddfc138faf068e77a40b7ce8ce4e0f135276b656466fa1f96"} {"question" "q-38248" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{1}{7}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14066271/state.json" "file_sha256" "d4617ec956bc238ddfc138faf068e77a40b7ce8ce4e0f135276b656466fa1f96"} {"question" "q-94389" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14066271/assets/c497efbb28bade962b997d7e14f5a272f0cead56f7567b1baaebae695c7c80f4.png)\n\nAn iron flat plate occupies the region $D$ enclosed between the curves $x=0,y=e,$ and $y={e}^{x},$ as shown above. The mass density function $\\lambda $ of the plate is given by $\\lambda (x,y)=\\frac{1}{y}.$ Find the total mass of the plate." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14066271/state.json" "file_sha256" "d4617ec956bc238ddfc138faf068e77a40b7ce8ce4e0f135276b656466fa1f96"} {"question" "q-94389" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The mass of the plate occupying the region $D$ with mass density $\\lambda (x,y)=\\frac{1}{y}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{1}{y}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $D$ is a type I region. \n\nNow, let's determine the limits of integration.\n\n- To determine the $x$-limits, we need to find the $x$-coordinates of the points at which the curves intersect. So, we set the following equation and solve it for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{x} & =e \\\\ x & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the $x$-limits are $0\\le x\\le 1.$\n\n- To determine the lower and upper functions, we draw a vertical arrow arrow through the region.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14066271/assets/95a6d9b0bc464c719311dc900967ebd06a3aa3d3004cfe5303359eecb20551e7.png)\n\nAs a result, we get\n\n$$\nD={(x,y):0\\le x\\le 1,\\,{e}^{x}\\le y\\le e}.\n$$\n\nSo, we can express the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}\\frac{1}{y}\\,\\text{d}A={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{{e}^{x}}^{e}\\frac{1}{y}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nFirst, we evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $y$, treating $x$ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}[{\\int }_{{e}^{x}}^{e}\\,\\text{d}y]\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}[\\operatorname{ln}⁡y]_{{e}^{x}}^{e}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡e-\\operatorname{ln}⁡{e}^{x})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(1-x)\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(1-x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={[x-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{{1}^{2}}{2}-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the total mass of the iron plate is $M=\\frac{1}{2}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14066271/state.json" "file_sha256" "d4617ec956bc238ddfc138faf068e77a40b7ce8ce4e0f135276b656466fa1f96"} {"question" "q-94389" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14066271/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "69c1da566dbedc787fd3cece82b0ecc66f9453f587bcf4e96a35c375c4cc13d7"} {"question" "q-94389" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14066271/state.json" "file_sha256" "d4617ec956bc238ddfc138faf068e77a40b7ce8ce4e0f135276b656466fa1f96"} {"question" "q-94389" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "1"] ["math" "2"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{2}"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{6}"] ["math" "\\frac{3}{2}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14066271/state.json" "file_sha256" "d4617ec956bc238ddfc138faf068e77a40b7ce8ce4e0f135276b656466fa1f96"} {"question" "q-94389" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{1}{2}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14066271/state.json" "file_sha256" "d4617ec956bc238ddfc138faf068e77a40b7ce8ce4e0f135276b656466fa1f96"} {"question" "q-38232" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14066271/assets/2d6dc14c5296291bffa9a942ef9465b27477ee4c58550faba507703c2781a54b.png)\n\nA thin sheet of caramel occupies the region $D$ enclosed between the curves $x=1,y=x$ and $y=2,$ as shown above. The mass density function $\\lambda $ of the sheet is given by $\\lambda (x,y)=\\frac{y}{x}.$ Find the total mass of the caramel sheet." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14066271/state.json" "file_sha256" "d4617ec956bc238ddfc138faf068e77a40b7ce8ce4e0f135276b656466fa1f96"} {"question" "q-38232" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The mass of the caramel sheet occupying the region $D$ with mass density $\\lambda (x,y)=\\frac{y}{x}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{y}{x}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $D$ is a type I region. \n\nNow, let's determine the limits of integration.\n\n- To determine the $x$-limits, we need to find the $x$-coordinates of the points at which the curves intersect. So, we set the following equation and solve it for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the $x$-limits are $1\\le x\\le 2.$\n\n- To determine the lower and upper functions, we draw a vertical arrow through the region.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14066271/assets/281ea7be1ef48c149bb4bcfd09aa41357caa8bb28adfc48285d298fb3cf89f3c.png)\n\nAs a result, we get\n\n$$\nD={(x,y):1\\le x\\le 2,\\,x\\le y\\le 2}.\n$$\n\nSo, we can express the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}\\frac{y}{x}\\,\\text{d}A={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{x}^{2}\\frac{y}{x}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nFirst, we evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $y$, treating $x$ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}[{\\int }_{x}^{2}\\frac{y}{x}\\,\\text{d}y]\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{1}{x}{[\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}]}_{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{1}{x}[\\frac{{2}^{2}}{2}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}]\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{2}{x}-\\frac{x}{2}\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{2}{x}-\\frac{x}{2}\\,\\text{d}x & ={[2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}]}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =(2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2-\\frac{{2}^{2}}{4})-(2\\operatorname{ln}⁡1-\\frac{{1}^{2}}{4}) \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2-\\frac{3}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the total mass of the ice sheet is $M=2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2-\\frac{3}{4}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14066271/state.json" "file_sha256" "d4617ec956bc238ddfc138faf068e77a40b7ce8ce4e0f135276b656466fa1f96"} {"question" "q-38232" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14066271/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "69c1da566dbedc787fd3cece82b0ecc66f9453f587bcf4e96a35c375c4cc13d7"} {"question" "q-38232" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14066271/state.json" "file_sha256" "d4617ec956bc238ddfc138faf068e77a40b7ce8ce4e0f135276b656466fa1f96"} {"question" "q-38232" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2"] ["math" "2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2+\\frac{3}{4}"] ["math" "2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2+\\frac{5}{4}"] ["math" "2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2-\\frac{3}{4}"] ["math" "2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2-\\frac{5}{4}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14066271/state.json" "file_sha256" "d4617ec956bc238ddfc138faf068e77a40b7ce8ce4e0f135276b656466fa1f96"} {"question" "q-38232" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2-\\frac{3}{4}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14066271/state.json" "file_sha256" "d4617ec956bc238ddfc138faf068e77a40b7ce8ce4e0f135276b656466fa1f96"} {"question" "q-94437" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A metal plate occupies the region $D={(x,y):0\\le x\\le \\sqrt{\\pi },\\,0\\le y\\le \\operatorname{sin}⁡{x}^{2}}.$ The charge density of the plate is given by $\\rho (x,y)=x.$ Find the total charge $Q$ of the plate." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14066271/state.json" "file_sha256" "d4617ec956bc238ddfc138faf068e77a40b7ce8ce4e0f135276b656466fa1f96"} {"question" "q-94437" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The total charge $Q$ of a plate that occupies the region $D$ with charge density $\\rho (x,y)=x$ is given by\n\n$$\nQ={∬}_{D}x\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nLet's sketch our region.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14066271/assets/2b179e35ae9af5a13d43c9a64bee99ee4ed87c257b623184f9165e14c24f3dee.png)\n\nNotice that $D$ is a type I region. So, we can express the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}x\\,\\text{d}A={\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{\\pi }}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡{x}^{2}}x\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nFirst, we evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $y$, treating $x$ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{\\pi }}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡{x}^{2}}x\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{\\pi }}[{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y]x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{\\pi }}[y]_{0}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡{x}^{2}}x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{\\pi }}x\\operatorname{sin}⁡{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{\\pi }}x\\operatorname{sin}⁡{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x & ={[-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡{x}^{2}]}_{0}^{\\sqrt{\\pi }} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}[\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi -\\operatorname{cos}⁡0] \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the total charge of the plate is $Q=1.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14066271/state.json" "file_sha256" "d4617ec956bc238ddfc138faf068e77a40b7ce8ce4e0f135276b656466fa1f96"} {"question" "q-94437" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14066271/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "69c1da566dbedc787fd3cece82b0ecc66f9453f587bcf4e96a35c375c4cc13d7"} {"question" "q-94437" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14066271/state.json" "file_sha256" "d4617ec956bc238ddfc138faf068e77a40b7ce8ce4e0f135276b656466fa1f96"} {"question" "q-94437" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "0"] ["math" "1"] ["math" "2"] ["math" "2\\pi "] ["math" "\\pi "]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14066271/state.json" "file_sha256" "d4617ec956bc238ddfc138faf068e77a40b7ce8ce4e0f135276b656466fa1f96"} {"question" "q-94437" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "1"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14066271/state.json" "file_sha256" "d4617ec956bc238ddfc138faf068e77a40b7ce8ce4e0f135276b656466fa1f96"} {"question" "q-94586" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14066271/assets/7583a2da0bff7d66a3512c58f67692100af403b61b19a9b52347519b30823342.png)\n\nFind the total charge $Q$ of a metal plate that occupies the region $D$ enclosed between  the parts of  the curves ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=4$ and ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1,$ as shown above, and has charge density $\\rho (x,y)=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14066271/state.json" "file_sha256" "d4617ec956bc238ddfc138faf068e77a40b7ce8ce4e0f135276b656466fa1f96"} {"question" "q-94586" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, notice that the region $D$ represents a half-annulus enclosed between the circles of the radiuses $1$ and $2$ centered at the origin.\n\nThe total charge $Q$ of an object occupying the region $D$ with charge density $\\rho (x,y)=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}$ is given by\n\n$$\nQ={∬}_{D}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe need to change the coordinate system from Cartesian coordinates to polar coordinates. To do this, we will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}\\rho (x,y)\\text{ }\\text{d}A={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }\\rho (r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nThe following equations connect Cartesian and polar coordinates:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write our curves in polar coordinates.\n\n- For ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=4,$ we obtain $r=2.$\n\n- For ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1,$ we obtain $r=1.$\n\nAs a result, the region can be expressed in polar coordinates $(r,\\theta )$ as \n\n$$\nΔ={(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,1\\le r\\le 2,\\,-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}}}\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{1}^{2}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}[r]_{1}^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}(2-1)\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =[\\theta ]_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}\\, \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}-(-\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the total charge of the object is $Q=\\pi .$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14066271/state.json" "file_sha256" "d4617ec956bc238ddfc138faf068e77a40b7ce8ce4e0f135276b656466fa1f96"} {"question" "q-94586" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14066271/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "69c1da566dbedc787fd3cece82b0ecc66f9453f587bcf4e96a35c375c4cc13d7"} {"question" "q-94586" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14066271/state.json" "file_sha256" "d4617ec956bc238ddfc138faf068e77a40b7ce8ce4e0f135276b656466fa1f96"} {"question" "q-94586" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-\\frac{\\pi }{2}"] ["math" "-\\pi "] ["math" "2\\pi "] ["math" "\\frac{\\pi }{2}"] ["math" "\\pi "]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14066271/state.json" "file_sha256" "d4617ec956bc238ddfc138faf068e77a40b7ce8ce4e0f135276b656466fa1f96"} {"question" "q-94586" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\pi "] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14066271/state.json" "file_sha256" "d4617ec956bc238ddfc138faf068e77a40b7ce8ce4e0f135276b656466fa1f96"} {"question" "q-94720" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14066271/assets/514a3cd39f24a7b2477e6214964d69cd743a26645eba7f4b797af832b565fa93.png)\n\nAn object occupies the region $D$ in the second quadrant enclosed between the curves ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1$ and ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=9$, as shown above. The mass density function $\\lambda $ of the object is given by $\\lambda (x,y)=y.$ Find the mass of the object." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14066271/state.json" "file_sha256" "d4617ec956bc238ddfc138faf068e77a40b7ce8ce4e0f135276b656466fa1f96"} {"question" "q-94720" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The mass of an object which occupies the region $D$ with mass density $\\lambda (x,y)=y$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ={∬}_{D}y\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe will convert this to polar coordinates using the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}\\lambda (x,y)\\text{ }\\text{d}A={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }\\lambda (r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nNotice that the region $D$ represents a portion between the disks of radii $1$ and $3$ centered at the origin. \n\nThe following equations connect cartesian and polar coordinates:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write our curves in polar coordinates.\n\n- For ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1,$ we obtain $r=1.$\n\n- For ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=9,$ we obtain $r=3.$\n\nNow, let's find the limits for the parameter $\\theta .$ The entire region lies between the radial lines $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ and $\\theta =\\pi ,$ as depicted below.  \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14066271/assets/7f3b2717946956ade07c252413130a730070e370cab85c73d866fa9506604106.png)\n\nAs a result, the region can be expressed in polar coordinates $(r,\\theta )$ as \n\n$$\nD={(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,1\\le r\\le 3,\\,\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le \\pi }.\n$$\n\nFinally, using the change of variables formula, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}y\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{1}^{3}{r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{1}^{3}{r}^{2}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {[\\frac{{r}^{3}}{3}]}_{1}^{3}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta (9-\\frac{1}{3})\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{26}{3}{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{26}{3}[-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ]_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{26}{3}(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi +\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{26}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the total mass of the object is $M=\\frac{26}{3}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14066271/state.json" "file_sha256" "d4617ec956bc238ddfc138faf068e77a40b7ce8ce4e0f135276b656466fa1f96"} {"question" "q-94720" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14066271/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "69c1da566dbedc787fd3cece82b0ecc66f9453f587bcf4e96a35c375c4cc13d7"} {"question" "q-94720" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14066271/state.json" "file_sha256" "d4617ec956bc238ddfc138faf068e77a40b7ce8ce4e0f135276b656466fa1f96"} {"question" "q-94720" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "26"] ["math" "3"] ["math" "\\frac{22}{3}"] ["math" "\\frac{26}{3}"] ["math" "\\frac{26}{5}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14066271/state.json" "file_sha256" "d4617ec956bc238ddfc138faf068e77a40b7ce8ce4e0f135276b656466fa1f96"} {"question" "q-94720" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{26}{3}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14066271/state.json" "file_sha256" "d4617ec956bc238ddfc138faf068e77a40b7ce8ce4e0f135276b656466fa1f96"} {"question" "q-94753" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the mass of an ice object which has the form of a quarter-disk, in the fourth quadrant, of radius $3$ centered at the origin and mass density function $\\lambda $ given by $\\lambda (x,y)=8({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14066271/state.json" "file_sha256" "d4617ec956bc238ddfc138faf068e77a40b7ce8ce4e0f135276b656466fa1f96"} {"question" "q-94753" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The mass of a quarter-disk $D$ of radius $3$ centered at the origin with mass density $\\lambda (x,y)=8({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ={∬}_{D}8({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}\\lambda (x,y)\\text{ }\\text{d}A={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }\\lambda (r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch our region $D:$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14066271/assets/4f7b1647d893512605ee1a2e9cee3e5511a00dc7ad9b1765fff1d0ba0710895f.png)\n\nIn polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ the region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ={(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 3,\\,-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le 0}.\n$$\n\nFinally, using the change of variables formula, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}8({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}8({r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{0}{\\int }_{0}^{3}8{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =8{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{0}[\\frac{{r}^{4}}{4}]_{0}^{3}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{0}(81-0)\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =162{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{0}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =162[\\theta ]_{-\\pi /2}^{0} \\\\  & =162(0-(-\\frac{\\pi }{2})) \\\\  & =81\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the total mass of the quarter-disk is $M=81\\pi .$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14066271/state.json" "file_sha256" "d4617ec956bc238ddfc138faf068e77a40b7ce8ce4e0f135276b656466fa1f96"} {"question" "q-94753" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14066271/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "69c1da566dbedc787fd3cece82b0ecc66f9453f587bcf4e96a35c375c4cc13d7"} {"question" "q-94753" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14066271/state.json" "file_sha256" "d4617ec956bc238ddfc138faf068e77a40b7ce8ce4e0f135276b656466fa1f96"} {"question" "q-94753" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "162\\pi "] ["math" "20"] ["math" "25\\pi "] ["math" "50\\pi "] ["math" "81\\pi "]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14066271/state.json" "file_sha256" "d4617ec956bc238ddfc138faf068e77a40b7ce8ce4e0f135276b656466fa1f96"} {"question" "q-94753" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "81\\pi "] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14066271/state.json" "file_sha256" "d4617ec956bc238ddfc138faf068e77a40b7ce8ce4e0f135276b656466fa1f96"} {"question" "q-38942" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the mass of a glass object which has the form of an upper half-disk of radius $3$ centered at the origin and mass density function $\\lambda $ given by $\\lambda (x,y)=y\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14066271/state.json" "file_sha256" "d4617ec956bc238ddfc138faf068e77a40b7ce8ce4e0f135276b656466fa1f96"} {"question" "q-38942" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The mass of an upper half-disk $D$ of radius $3$ centered at the origin with mass density $\\lambda (x,y)=y\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ={∬}_{D}y\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}\\lambda (x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }\\lambda (r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch our region $D:$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14066271/assets/d8e2f73ce8840471143689748e188debaf5fde8f3aaf5021fd8d99c4f86a72d6.png)\n\nIn polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ the region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ={(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 3,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le \\pi }.\n$$\n\nFinally, using the change of variables formula, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}y\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\sqrt{(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}}\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{3}{r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\sqrt{{r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{3}{r}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{0}^{3}{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {[\\frac{{r}^{4}}{4}]}_{0}^{3}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta (\\frac{81}{4}-0)\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{81}{4}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{81}{4}[-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ]_{0}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{81}{4}(-(-1)+1) \\\\  & =\\frac{81}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the total mass of the upper half-disk is $M=\\frac{81}{2}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14066271/state.json" "file_sha256" "d4617ec956bc238ddfc138faf068e77a40b7ce8ce4e0f135276b656466fa1f96"} {"question" "q-38942" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14066271/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "69c1da566dbedc787fd3cece82b0ecc66f9453f587bcf4e96a35c375c4cc13d7"} {"question" "q-38942" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14066271/state.json" "file_sha256" "d4617ec956bc238ddfc138faf068e77a40b7ce8ce4e0f135276b656466fa1f96"} {"question" "q-38942" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\frac{27}{2}"] ["math" "\\frac{27}{4}"] ["math" "\\frac{27}{8}"] ["math" "\\frac{81}{2}"] ["math" "\\frac{81}{4}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14066271/state.json" "file_sha256" "d4617ec956bc238ddfc138faf068e77a40b7ce8ce4e0f135276b656466fa1f96"} {"question" "q-38942" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{81}{2}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14066271/state.json" "file_sha256" "d4617ec956bc238ddfc138faf068e77a40b7ce8ce4e0f135276b656466fa1f96"} {"question" "q-94750" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the mass of a plastic object which has the form of a lower half-disk of radius $4$ centered at the origin and mass density function $\\lambda $ given by $\\lambda (x,y)={y}^{2}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14066271/state.json" "file_sha256" "d4617ec956bc238ddfc138faf068e77a40b7ce8ce4e0f135276b656466fa1f96"} {"question" "q-94750" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The mass of a lower half-disk $D$ of radius $4$ centered at the origin with mass density $\\lambda (x,y)=y$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ={∬}_{D}y\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}\\lambda (x,y)\\text{ }\\text{d}A={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }\\lambda (r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch our region $D:$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14066271/assets/379073190f15a5e80c696b8f5bb77132dd515ca2493b8f0407354bbac923bb91.png)\n\nIn polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ the region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ={(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 4,\\,\\pi \\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi }.\n$$\n\nFinally, using the change of variables formula, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}y\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{4}{r}^{3}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{0}^{4}{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta [\\frac{{r}^{4}}{4}]_{0}^{4}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta (64-0)\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =64{\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =32{\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =32[\\theta -\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta }{2}]_{\\pi }^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =32\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the total mass of the upper half-disk is $M=32\\pi .$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14066271/state.json" "file_sha256" "d4617ec956bc238ddfc138faf068e77a40b7ce8ce4e0f135276b656466fa1f96"} {"question" "q-94750" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14066271/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "69c1da566dbedc787fd3cece82b0ecc66f9453f587bcf4e96a35c375c4cc13d7"} {"question" "q-94750" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14066271/state.json" "file_sha256" "d4617ec956bc238ddfc138faf068e77a40b7ce8ce4e0f135276b656466fa1f96"} {"question" "q-94750" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "16\\pi "] ["math" "256\\pi "] ["math" "32\\pi "] ["math" "64\\pi "] ["math" "68\\pi "]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14066271/state.json" "file_sha256" "d4617ec956bc238ddfc138faf068e77a40b7ce8ce4e0f135276b656466fa1f96"} {"question" "q-94750" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "32\\pi "] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14066271/state.json" "file_sha256" "d4617ec956bc238ddfc138faf068e77a40b7ce8ce4e0f135276b656466fa1f96"} {"question" "e-8193" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the total charge $Q$ of a metal plate that occupies the region $D = \\{ (x,y) : 0 \\le x \\le 1, \\, 0 \\le y \\le 2\\}$ with charge density $\\rho (x,y) = 4 - 3y^2 + 2x.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14066271/example-8193.json" "file_sha256" "137bd18e0ffc79b4e52724278dd2ba19cc082641236b716509774d9be84b7649"} {"question" "e-8193" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The total charge $Q$ of a plate that occupies the region $D = \\{ (x,y) : 0 \\leq x \\leq 1, \\, 0 \\leq y \\leq 2\\}$ with charge density $\\rho (x,y) = 4 - 3y^2 + 2x$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q & ={∬}_{D}(4-3{y}^{2}+2x)\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we express our double integral as a repeated integral:\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_{D} (4 - 3y^2 + 2x) \\:\\textrm{d}A\n= \\int_{0}^2 \\left[ \\int_{0}^1 (4 - 3y^2 + 2x) \\:\\textrm{d}x \\right] \\textrm{d}y\n$$\n\nNow, we evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $x$, treating $y$ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{2}[{\\int }_{0}^{1}(4-3{y}^{2}+2x)\\,\\text{d}x]\\text{d}y & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}[4x-3{y}^{2}x+{x}^{2}]_{0}^{1}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}[(4(1)-3{y}^{2}(1)+(1)^{2})-(4(0)-3{y}^{2}(0)-(0)^{2})]\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}(5-3{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we integrate with respect to $y{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{2}(5-3{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}y & =[5y-{y}^{3}]_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =(5(2)-(2)^{3})-(5(0)-(0)^{3}) \\\\  & =(10-8)-0 \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the charge of the plate is $Q=2.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14066271/example-8193.json" "file_sha256" "137bd18e0ffc79b4e52724278dd2ba19cc082641236b716509774d9be84b7649"} {"question" "e-8192" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14066271/assets/73e2bd9a47c9d16f93d5240db3c4babc36f9fbe703ac9e0af449acaa7528f804.png)\n\nA triangular plate occupies the region ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14066271/assets/6e8362574defb66da2f3a81265f216d5e82fe6f01d9db6dc6fde3879b58d137a.png) enclosed between the lines ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14066271/assets/0b759759dc6e40e6a983b68c1b1ac821c304183d638e47347d3dc15bb6d43711.png) and ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14066271/assets/e5edcbd505cc63ee5461688fda2a101b0e6d529c2cb8185906f9ee564108415d.png) as shown above. The plate's mass density function ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14066271/assets/8cd15d36128c5a26b2b25f57faa5cc41ecbe3e94af66e9c053a3da0a5425bac4.png) is given by ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14066271/assets/d83addab9edd09882eb2af07d7ceb9facb3c4ca85179e3ad771581f00e3c932f.png) Find the total mass of the triangular plate." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14066271/example-8192.json" "file_sha256" "01c33c94ed082a39493c9adab4a9a5b779bb634a2e524e332be3e94635fca389"} {"question" "e-8192" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The mass of the triangular plate occupying the region ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14066271/assets/3c51a5e31ce9f37895e4e38935b015ba8fd40373baa1c8735bd63f3592968af9.png) with mass density ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14066271/assets/ca272ba1efbdf1cd3d7c6c0d41a91233ce4a64659e5c96d040f9ff2da45b6ddf.png) is given by\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14066271/assets/457c03e2f0a2295eaf404978491ea77c038507ba33bb1125a2a05952d0565af3.png)\n\nNotice that ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14066271/assets/05ff793b0aee1f05a79216f9e7f119afa544749c81077937f8f06a353f6ef4eb.png) is a type I region. So, we can express the integral as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14066271/assets/4c3142a8a4beae1e14a9ca2a611a7101d9a2a4cfe553109ae20a1d1b0dd78f64.png)\n\nFirst, we evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14066271/assets/d2a923f8537e18206a972cc41916ae6f9ee26abc93734cd0a73c50ffc165e08a.png) treating ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14066271/assets/0ea8d498dfe5ba44cab2ca474bceae4ef3205271d683d0fdeedf7ffd68a22a43.png) as a constant:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14066271/assets/a9b1d2210f33a1242fccf6517fbc63783381f19cdc48daf78685900497b0829f.png)\n\nThen, we integrate with respect to ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14066271/assets/73c3b5d4a287ad14dceb771dbd299c9effc9f3aff16c0f4b4051737ed99b36ae.png)\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14066271/assets/3e75cffa3638e6260cbe5493a8277a484fe594d41e20ad55588da0b2dcf21fca.png)\n\nTherefore, the total mass of the plate is ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14066271/assets/327bb4cc2df638a85e6bbe52eeda604b942f097b691c0c2125d579596dfe54a8.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14066271/example-8192.json" "file_sha256" "01c33c94ed082a39493c9adab4a9a5b779bb634a2e524e332be3e94635fca389"} {"question" "e-8195" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14066271/assets/f0db928874dba8d872cde46d1467686010f3484765b2d7e39807b5a32a131b09.png)\n\nFind the total charge $Q$ of a copper plate that occupies the region $D$ shown above, and has charge density \n\n$$\n\\rho (x,y) = \\dfrac{4x}{\\sqrt{x^2+y^2}}  .\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14066271/example-8195.json" "file_sha256" "ec41184e5f4c0d0e6f830761ca15232ddfd78207754d4060bb9ff39babbdb20f"} {"question" "e-8195" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, notice that the region $D$ represents one-quarter of the disk of radius $3$ centered at \n$\\left(0,0\\right).$\n\nThe total charge $Q$ of an object occupying the region $D$ with charge density $\\rho (x,y) =  \\dfrac{4x}{\\sqrt{x^2+y^2}}$ is given by\n\n$$\nQ = \\iint\\limits_{D} \\dfrac{4x}{\\sqrt{x^2+y^2}}  \\,\\textrm{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe need to change the coordinate system from Cartesian coordinates to polar coordinates. To do this, we will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_{D} \\rho(x,y) \\ \\mathrm d A = \\iint\\limits_{\\Delta} \\ \\rho(r \\cos\\theta, r\\sin\\theta) \\:  r \\ \\mathrm d r \\mathrm d \\theta.\n$$\n\nThe following equations connect Cartesian and polar coordinates:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write the curve $x^2+y^2=9$ in polar coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =9 \\\\ (r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2} & =9 \\\\ {r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta ) & =9 \\\\ {r}^{2} & =9 \\\\ r & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, in polar coordinates $(r, \\theta),$ the region can be expressed as \n\n$$\n\\Delta = \\Big\\{ (r,\\theta) \\: : \\: 0 \\leq r \\leq 3, \\: \\pi \\leq \\theta \\leq \\dfrac{3\\pi}{2} \\Big\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\frac{4x}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}\\frac{4r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\sqrt{{r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }}\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi }^{3\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{3}4r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi }^{3\\pi /2}4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta [{\\int }_{0}^{3}r\\,\\text{d}r]\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi }^{3\\pi /2}4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta {[\\frac{{r}^{2}}{2}]}_{0}^{3}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi }^{3\\pi /2}4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta (\\frac{9}{2}-0)\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =18{\\int }_{\\pi }^{3\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =18[\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ]_{\\pi }^{3\\pi /2} \\\\  & =18[-1-0] \\\\  & =-18.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the total charge of the quarter-disk is $Q = - 18.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14066271/example-8195.json" "file_sha256" "ec41184e5f4c0d0e6f830761ca15232ddfd78207754d4060bb9ff39babbdb20f"}] "usage" {"e-8192" [] "e-8193" [] "e-8195" [] "q-38248" []} "basis" 2181 "retractions" [[17592186129574 :question/problem "A metal plate occupies the region $D = {(x, y): - 1 \\le y \\le 1, 0 \\le x \\le y^{2}}$. The charge density of the plate is given by $ρ(x, y) = xy^{2}$. Find the total charge $Q$ of the plate."] [17592186128963 :question/problem "Find the total charge $Q$ of a metal plate that occupies the region $D = \\{(x,y): 0 \\le x \\le 1, \\, 0 \\le y \\le 2\\}$ with charge density $\\rho (x,y) = 4 - 3y^2 + 2x$."] [17592186128963 :question/worked-solution "The total charge $Q$ of a plate that occupies the region\n\n$$\nD = \\{(x,y): 0 \\leq x \\leq 1, \\, 0 \\leq y \\leq 2\\}\n$$\n\nwith charge density\n\n$$\n\\rho (x,y) = 4 - 3y^2 + 2x\n$$\n\nis given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nQ &= ∬_(D)(4 - 3y^{2} + 2x)dA\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we express our double integral as a repeated integral:\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_{D} (4 - 3y^2 + 2x) \\:\\textrm{d}A = \\int_{0}^2 \\left[ \\int_{0}^1 (4 - 3y^2 + 2x) \\:\\textrm{d}x \\right] \\textrm{d}y\n$$\n\nNow, we evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $x$, treating $y$ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∫_{0}^{2}[∫_{0}^{1}(4 - 3y^{2} + 2x)dx]dy &= ∫_{0}^{2}[4x - 3y^{2}x + x^{2}]_{0}^{1}dy \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{2}[(4(1) - 3y^{2}(1) + (1)^{2}) - (4(0) - 3y^{2}(0) - (0)^{2})]dy \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{2}(5 - 3y^{2})dy\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we integrate with respect to $y{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∫_{0}^{2}(5 - 3y^{2})dy &= [5y - y^{3}]_{0}^{2} \\\\\n&= (5(2) - (2)^{3}) - (5(0) - (0)^{3}) \\\\\n&= (10 - 8) - 0 \\\\\n&= 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the charge of the plate is $Q=2$."] [17592186128962 :question/problem "A triangular plate occupies the region $D$ enclosed between the lines $x=1,y=0$ and $y=x$, as shown above. The plate's mass density function $\\lambda$ is given by $\\lambda(x,y) = xy^2$. Find the total mass of the triangular plate.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2025/Source/Images/f15db6d8baa8892b061cfaa81871fd0e.png)"] [17592186128962 :question/worked-solution "The mass of the triangular plate occupying the region $D$ with mass density\n\n$$\n\\lambda(x,y) = x^2y\n$$\n\nis given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nM &= ∬_(D)xy^{2}dA\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $D$ is a type I region. So, we can express the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\iint \\limits_{D} xy^2\\,\\mathrm{d}A = \\int_{0}^1 \\int_{0}^{x} xy^2\\,\\mathrm{d}y \\, \\mathrm{d}x\n$$\n\nFirst, we evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $y$, treating $x$ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nM &= ∫_{0}^{1}x[∫_{0}^{x}y^{2}dy]dx \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{1}x[\\frac{y^{3}}{3}]_{0}^{x}dx \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{1}x(\\frac{x^{3}}{3} - 0)dx \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{3}∫_{0}^{1}x^{4}dx\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate with respect to $x{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{1}{3}∫_{0}^{1}x^{4}dx &= \\frac{1}{3}[\\frac{x^{5}}{5}]_{0}^{1} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{3}(\\frac{1}{5} - 0) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{15}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the total mass of the plate is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{15}\n$$"] [17592186128964 :question/problem "Find the total charge $Q$ of a copper plate that occupies the region $D$ shown above, and has charge density $\\rho (x,y) = \\dfrac{4x}{\\sqrt{x^2+y^2}}$.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2025/Source/Images/c3d92d5e0c81c1871fad053bd3726a50.png)"] [17592186128964 :question/worked-solution "First, notice that the region $D$ represents one-quarter of the disk of radius $3$ centered at\n\n$$\n\\left(0,0\\right)\n$$\n\nThe total charge $Q$ of an object occupying the region $D$ with charge density\n\n$$\n\\rho (x,y) = \\dfrac{4x}{\\sqrt{x^2+y^2}}\n$$\n\nis given by\n\n$$\nQ = \\iint\\limits_{D} \\dfrac{4x}{\\sqrt{x^2+y^2}} \\,\\textrm{d}A\n$$\n\nWe need to change the coordinate system from Cartesian coordinates to polar coordinates. To do this, we will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_{D} \\rho(x,y) \\ \\mathrm d A = \\iint\\limits_{\\Delta} \\ \\rho(r \\cos\\theta, r\\sin\\theta) \\: r \\ \\mathrm d r \\mathrm d \\theta\n$$\n\nThe following equations connect Cartesian and polar coordinates:\n\n$$\n{x = r\\cos θ; y = r\\sin θ\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write the curve\n\n$$\nx^2+y^2=9\n$$\n\nin polar coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^{2} + y^{2} &= 9 \\\\\n(r\\cos θ)^{2} + (r\\sin θ)^{2} &= 9 \\\\\nr^{2}(\\cos^{2} θ + \\sin^{2} θ) &= 9 \\\\\nr^{2} &= 9 \\\\\nr &= 3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, in polar coordinates $(r, \\theta)$, the region can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\Delta = \\Big\\{(r,\\theta) \\:: \\: 0 \\leq r \\leq 3, \\: \\pi \\leq \\theta \\leq \\dfrac{3\\pi}{2} \\Big\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∬_(D)\\frac{4x}{\\sqrt{x^{2} + y^{2}}}dA &= ∬_(Δ)\\frac{4r\\cos θ}{\\sqrt{r^{2}\\cos^{2} θ + r^{2}\\sin^{2} θ}}rdrdθ \\\\\n&= ∫_{π}^{3π/2}∫_{0}^{3}4r\\cos θdrdθ \\\\\n&= ∫_{π}^{3π/2}4\\cos θ[∫_{0}^{3}rdr]dθ \\\\\n&= ∫_{π}^{3π/2}4\\cos θ[\\frac{r^{2}}{2}]_{0}^{3}dθ \\\\\n&= ∫_{π}^{3π/2}4\\cos θ(\\frac{9}{2} - 0)dθ \\\\\n&= 18∫_{π}^{3π/2}\\cos θdθ \\\\\n&= 18[\\sin θ]_{π}^{3π/2} \\\\\n&= 18[- 1 - 0] \\\\\n&=-18\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the total charge of the quarter-disk is $Q =-18$."]] "immutable_answers" [17592186127239 17592186127240 17592186127241 17592186127242 17592186127243] "immutable_fields" [[17592186127238 :answer-field/correct] [17592186127238 :answer-field/id] [17592186127238 :answer-field/key] [17592186127238 :answer-field/type]] "no_history" []}
